  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM EDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nA complex situation of various boundaries, stable layers, and upper \nlevel forcing lead to low confidence in the details of how \nconvection will evolve through the rest of the afternoon and \nevening. A linear boundary is evident in satellite, stretching from \nnear Nashville to our KY/VA border counties, with another boundary \nlocated farther north across south central and eastern KY. \nConvective towers are noted in the KY/VA border area with these \nboundaries. Morning convection in NE TN has created a thermal \nboundary between Knoxville and Morristown from storm outflow and \ndifferential heating from cloud cover. Satellite shows clearing \nnortheast of this boundary, so the air mass in NE TN and SW VA \nshould recover most its instability. South and west of this \nboundary, the air mass appears to be capped. Aloft, an upper jet \nnose is over our NE sections, with 500 mb vorticity in that area as \nwell. \n\nThe bottom line of these features is that a second round of \nshowers/storms is expected in our NE sections where the upper \nforcing and surface boundaries can interact. Bulk effective shear of \n35-40 kt and MLCAPE of 2000-3000 J/kg this afternoon will mean a \nthreat of severe storms, with damaging winds as the primary threat, \nand a lesser but non-zero threat of large hail and an isolated \ntornado. Some of this activity could make it into the central TN \nValley, if mesoscale forcing from outflow boundaries can overcome \nmidlevel capping - the southern and western extent of convection is \na big question mark. Again, the main threat area is likely east of I-\n75 and north of I-40. Flash flooding will be a concern where \ntraining of storms occurrs, especially in NE TN/SW VA where 1-hr FFG \nis only 1.3-1.5 inches. Storm motion from NW to SE may align with \nresidual boundaries from the morning convection, leading to training \ncells with high rainfall rates. \n\nWednesday through Friday will feature some very pleasant conditions \nfor the start of August. With a trough over the Atlantic coast and a \nnortherly flow across our area, temperatures will drop slightly \nbelow normal. Humidity will also trend lower into Thursday before a \ntrend upward begins Friday - afternoon dewpoints will be in the 60s, \nand some low temps in the 50s will be possible in northern sections. \n\nA wet pattern may develop again over the weekend as a closed upper \nlow drops SW from the upper Plains into the Mid MS-OH Valley region \nand stays parked to our west through the rest of the forecast \nperiod. A deep southerly flow from the Gulf will provide rain \nchances each day from Saturday through Tuesday.",
